{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0ef5fc1c76aa6126ed083e6bd9d6d25e2a2f833c","text":"Retiring my own probe record, and recording what it found, which is more important than the record: THE WRITE-BOUNDARY GUARD IS MERGED BUT NOT IN FORCE.\n\nWHAT I DID AND WHY. Writing the reserved-fields documentation for e17b7088, I did not want to describe a refusal I had only read in source. So I deliberately filed an assert carrying body.review_path, expecting the admission boundary to refuse it before signing and nothing to reach the log.\n\nIT LANDED. The act was recorded and ruled effective, with body.review_path intact. Had I published the page on the strength of my code reading, it would have stated a refusal that does not happen.\n\nWHY IT LANDED, established rather than guessed. The guard merged into main at 27478dc3 on 2026-08-27 at 06:55:08. The resident serving 127.0.0.1:7777 — the one every MCP write in this workroom goes through — started on 2026-08-26 at 15:57:56 and has been up for fifteen and a half hours. It predates the guard by about fifteen hours and is still executing the pre-guard binary. So internal/app/admission.go refuses reserved fields in main, and refuses nothing in the process actually accepting our writes.\n\nI nearly missed this. go version -m ./bin/gs reports 27478dc3, because I rebuilt that file an hour ago. It reads the file on disk, not the image a running process loaded fifteen hours earlier. A binary's recorded revision says nothing about what is running.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS RIGHT NOW. Every durable write by planner, claude and codex through the resident is still passing the old, unguarded boundary. Undefined kinds, verdict-shaped generic state, dead bases and reserved fields are all admitted exactly as they were yesterday. Anyone who tests guard behaviour against the live system will conclude it does not work; anyone who assumes it works because it merged is also wrong. The guard's own tests pass, its review was sound, and the merge was correct — the gap is purely deployment.\n\nWHAT SHOULD HAPPEN. The resident on 7777 and the chess resident on 7778 need restarting on the current binary, and the MCP adapters with them. I am not restarting anything: those processes serve three actors and one of them may be mid-write, and killing another actor's writer without asking is not mine to do. Hugh or whoever owns that deployment should decide when.\n\nWHAT I AM DOING ABOUT MY OWN LANE. The documentation stands as written, because it describes main and main is what it describes. But I will say in the artifact that the behaviour requires a resident running at or after 27478dc3, so a reader who tries it against a stale resident and sees their reserved field accepted knows which of the two is wrong.\n\nThis probe record is retired because it says nothing anyone needs; the finding is here instead."}